# Private storage paths are declared, and declared paths are never widened

## Why

The deploy and rollback make the whole storage tree 2775/0664 so the runtime can
work, then strip "other" only from `DMS_PRIVATE_PATHS`. That list was written
before the clinical-evidence, legacy-odontogram and legacy-mass-upload disks
existed, so every deploy left those disks — and two old dump directories —
readable by every local account on the host. HTTP was never involved; the
exposure was at the filesystem level. The isolation verifier checked the same
list, so it reported GO.

A second defect hid behind the first: the deploy sources the runtime identity
file from the live host copy *before* it fast-forwards. Adding a path to the
list in a release only protected it from the next deploy on, and the deploy
shipping the change would itself widen the path.

## Rules

1. **Every private local disk is listed.** A disk in `config/filesystems.php`
   with `driver => local`, a root under `storage/`, and a visibility other than
   `public` must be covered by `DMS_PRIVATE_PATHS`.
   `RuntimeIdentityIsolationPrivatePathsTest` fails until it is.
2. **Dump and evidence-manifest directories are listed too**, even when no disk
   points at them: `storage/app/backups`, `storage/app/deploy-backups`,
   `storage/backups`, `storage/app/clinical-evidence-migration`, `storage/logs`.
3. **Private trees are never widened.** Both normalization paths prune every
   declared path from the 2775/0664 `find`, alongside the existing backup-tree
   prune, then give the owner read/write (`u+rwX`) and strip "other" (`o-rwx`).
   Group bits are left as found; the runtime group has no other member.
4. **The deploy merges, never replaces.** After `git merge --ff-only` it reads
   the target commit's `DMS_PRIVATE_PATHS` from the pinned object and takes the
   union with the live list (`dms_private_paths_union`). A release can add a
   private path; it can never drop one the host protects. An invalid entry fails
   the deploy closed.
5. **The verifier checks the merged set.** The deploy passes it with
   `--additional-private-paths`; the option only adds checks, and an invalid
   entry is a FAIL.
6. **One definition of a valid path.** `scripts/lib/private-paths.sh`: relative,
   under `storage/`, plain segments, no `..`, no glob characters. Lists are split
   with `read -a`, never by unquoted expansion.
7. **A git revert reopens the exposure.** The deploy that lands a revert still
   restricts the paths (live list), but later deploys would not. Re-contain
   instead of assuming the revert is safe.
8. **Never widen a private tree by hand**, and never fix an access problem by
   adding "other" bits; the runtime owns these trees.

## Not covered here

- Environment-file copies are governed by
  `100-production-secret-file-permission-hardening.mdc`; files named outside its
  backup patterns are treated as runtime-readable (0640, runtime group only).
- One-time host provisioning (`scripts/provision-runtime-identity.sh`) still
  widens before restricting; it is not part of the deploy path.
